This is a micropython directory.

The code here must be run in micropython either on local host using mpremote OR uploaded to an ESP32 device.
The Adc_async_client is remote and battery powered.
Its purpose is to measure and report on a 3-pack of 18650 Lithium Ion battery cells.
Voltage taps on the cells provides three voltage channels: One cell, Two cells, Three cells.
A fourth channel is used for current measurement. Its FSR=1.024V. The measured voltage is Vs (across Rs ~ 50mΩ)
The server can compute the voltages for each of the three cells in isolation by  
subtraction of the voltage measurements. Letting  Vc = cell voltage and Vt = tap voltage, 
     Vc0=Vt0 - GND, Vc1=Vt1-Vt0,  Vc2 = Vt2-Vt1. 
The Adc_async_client receives commands via asyncio_connection from the Server running in Python3
The commands originate from the Gui_asyncio_client running on any display host running in Python3. 
The Server receives the commands via asyncio from the Gui_asyncio_client and forwards them to 
Adc_async_client after adding a unique msgid from a db table (MSGID) into the dict/msg. (table acts as a generator)
Upon receiving the command, the  Adc_async_client performs a measurement and stores the A2D samples (currently 64) in memory. 
Upon the submission of the voltage_report, adc object builds/formats the voltage_report_msg, which is json.dumps(voltage_report_msg) + "\n".
Then it sends via asyncio_connection a message to the Server in which is embedded the A2D samples and other needed fields.
A measurement command (30) will result in 4 channels in a measurement report, each with voltage measurements for 
( One cell , Two Cells, Three Cells) respectively. A fourth channel is used to send measurement of amps over the period.
A calibration command(40) will be for four channels and will contain the Truth values: vins=[Vin0,Vin1,Vin2], (input voltages=tapVoltages). 
A calibration command will result in one calibration_report(41)with 4 channels: (0,1,2,amps) in a msg sent to the Server with 
"vins" embedded along with normal samples and neccessary fields.
The Adc_async_client can receive commands: 
   Measure                     # (30), 
   Calibrate                   # (40), 
   Start_Perioc_measurements   # (32), 
   Start_Periodic_Calibrations # (42), 
   sync_time                   # (3). 
For periodic , the Gui_async_client will embed two attributes in msgs thru the Server to the Adc_async_client, Start_Periodic.period and Start_Periodic.repetitions.
Once Periodic_Measurements are started, Adc_async_client just measures and reports at the specified periods  until the requested repetitions are fulfilled.
A measurement(30) and a calibration(40) command will vary in two ways: 
   1. the type ('m','c') where 'm' means measure and 'c' means calibrate
   2. vins = [Vin0, Vin1, Vin2] truthValues for a calibration. For a measurement, vins=[None, None, None] 
The time expended in ADC client will be ~1 second per channel, or about 3 seconds for all three.
A small amount of time will be expended in translating incoming msgs and formatting outgoing messages.

Summary:
1. Code must run in micropython.(mpremote or uploaded to ESP32) 
2. ADC Client must be able to receive commands via json and asyncio
3. ADC Client must be able to report back to Server via json and asyncio.
4. Future: Client must be flexible as to the number of samples, sample_rate, etc.
5. Process latency estimated to be less than 5 seconds round trip from GUI_client -> Svr ->ADC_client.-> Svr -> DB table -> GUI_client.
6. Msg sizes should target less than 1kb. This may be larger as experience indicates.

